--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -51,3 +51,26 @@ result of the previous one. The document must be a JSON value too. If the patch
 malformed, or an operation cannot be applied (a `test` that does not match, a `remove` or `replace` of a
 missing value, a `move` of a value into one of its own children, and so on), `applyPatch` throws a
 `JsonPatchError` and returns nothing, so a failed patch never leaves a partial result behind.
+
+## JSON Merge Patch
+
+`applyMergePatch(target, patch)` applies a JSON Merge Patch ([RFC 7386](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7386)),
+a JSON document that describes a partial update of another one. It is the simpler alternative to JSON Patch
+for clients that only send the members they change: an object in the patch merges into the target member by
+member, a member set to `null` removes the member of the same name, and any other value replaces the target
+as a whole:
+
+```ts
+import { applyMergePatch } from 'json-toolkit';
+
+const document = { title: 'Draft', tags: ['a'], owner: { name: 'Ada', email: 'ada@example.com' } };
+
+applyMergePatch(document, { title: 'Final', tags: ['b'], owner: { email: null } });
+// { title: 'Final', tags: ['b'], owner: { name: 'Ada' } }
+```
+
+Arrays are replaced, never merged element by element, as RFC 7386 requires. The result is a new value:
+`target` and `patch` are never modified, and the result shares no object with either of them. Both must be
+JSON values (no `undefined`, no array hole, no cycle, no object other than a plain one), otherwise
+`applyMergePatch` throws a `JsonMergePatchError`. A merge patch cannot set a member to `null`: `null` always
+means removal, so a client that needs to store `null` must use JSON Patch instead.
